S = 'js1-asset-checkout'; P = fe.REG[S]['abs']; os.chdir(P); done = ['review#1 fixture title corrected (P1 1.4); review#4/#5 test formatting, the executable-examples sync and the leaked paragraph were handled by the sweep']

def rewrite02(t):
    return fe.replace_section(t, r'Identity, representation and derived values', """Two representation bugs lived in this project before the change, and both are worth more than a footnote.

**The seed that silently nulled every date.** `server/db/schema.sql` seeds from JSON with `json_to_recordset(...) AS x(..., due_date text, ...)`. `json_to_recordset` matches JSON keys to column names *by name*, and the JSON key is `dueDate`. PostgreSQL folds unquoted identifiers to lower case, so `due_date` never matched `dueDate` and every seeded `due_date` was `NULL`; nothing failed, the column was just empty. The fix is the quoted identifier you now see: `"dueDate" text` in the record definition and `"dueDate"::date` in the `SELECT`. Rule: when a name comes from outside SQL, quote it.

**The date that became a timestamp.** `pg` parses the `date` type (OID 1082) into a JavaScript `Date` at local midnight, so `2028-02-29` read back on a machine west of UTC became `2028-02-28T...`. `server/src/db.ts:6` `pg.types.setTypeParser(1082, value => value)` turns that off: a date column now arrives as the string `2028-02-29`, which is what the API promised. Before the parser: `SELECT due_date FROM items` -> `2028-02-29T05:00:00.000Z` (in JavaScript); after: `'2028-02-29'`. The first test's `dueDate` round-trip assertion is what pins it.""") or t
fe.rw(fe.chapter(S, '02'), rewrite02); done.append('review#2 02 section on the json_to_recordset case-folding seed bug and the DATE type parser, replacing a generic section')
